---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import PdfSecureMarkdownToWord from '@/components/tools/data/PdfSecureMarkdownToWord.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="markdown-to-word">
  <PdfSecureMarkdownToWord client:load source="markdown" />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <section>
      <h2>Why this exists</h2>
      <p>
        Most technical writing is now in Markdown: READMEs, notes, documentation, and chatbot output.
        Organisations still use Word. A paste into Word usually means twenty minutes to fix headings
        that arrived as plain bold text and bullets that arrived as literal hyphens.
      </p>
      <p>
        This Markdown to Word converter writes a real .docx: a zip with
        <code>[Content_Types].xml</code>, <code>word/document.xml</code>, a styles part and a
        numbering part, in the same OOXML structure that Word produces.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What maps to what</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Markdown</th>
              <th scope="col">Word</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><code># … ######</code></td>
              <td>Heading 1 to Heading 6, with real outline levels</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>**bold**</code>, <code>*italic*</code>, <code>~~strike~~</code></td>
              <td>Character formatting on the run</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>`inline code`</code></td>
              <td>The HTML Code character style: Consolas on a grey background</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Fenced code blocks</td>
              <td>HTML Preformatted paragraphs, one per line, shaded</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>- item</code> / <code>1. item</code></td>
              <td>List Paragraph with a real numbering definition, three levels deep</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>- [x] task</code></td>
              <td>A bulleted item prefixed with ☑ or ☐</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>&gt; quote</code></td>
              <td>The Quote style: indented, italic, with a left rule</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>[text](url)</code></td>
              <td>A real hyperlink with a relationship entry, clickable in Word</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>GFM pipe tables</td>
              <td>A Table Grid table with a bold, repeating header row</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>---</code></td>
              <td>A bottom paragraph border</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>![alt](src)</code></td>
              <td>An italic <em>[image: alt]</em> placeholder. See below</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Styles, not hard formatting</h2>
      <p>
        A heading written as "18 pt bold dark blue" looks the same as Heading 1, but with hard
        formatting:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>References → Table of Contents</strong> finds nothing, because a TOC is built from
          outline levels.
        </li>
        <li>
          The navigation pane is empty, so you cannot jump through a long document.
        </li>
        <li>
          Your organisation's template does nothing, because there are no styles to change.
        </li>
        <li>
          Accessibility checkers flag the document, and screen readers cannot skim it by heading.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Export to PDF</strong> gives no bookmarks.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        This converter uses Word's built-in style ids, so all five work when the file opens.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The AI-output workflow</h2>
      <p>
        A common case: a chatbot gave a useful answer, and it must become a document. A sensible
        sequence:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>Ask for the answer in Markdown. Most models do this consistently.</li>
        <li>Copy the whole reply, including the heading hierarchy and any tables.</li>
        <li>Paste it here and set a document title, which also names the downloaded file.</li>
        <li>
          Open the .docx and apply your organisation's template. The headings are real styles, so the
          whole document changes style at once.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Note: this conversion is local, but the text you pasted into the chatbot already went to that
        company. If the source material was confidential, that happened before this page.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Images and other limits</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Images are placeholders. An <code>![alt](url)</code> almost always points to a remote
          address, and this page makes no network requests. The tool marks the position, and you
          insert the image yourself.
        </li>
        <li>
          Raw HTML in the Markdown becomes its text only. Use <a href="/html-to-word">HTML to Word</a>
          if HTML is the main source.
        </li>
        <li>
          Footnotes and definition lists are not part of core GFM and become ordinary paragraphs.
        </li>
        <li>
          Nested lists work to three levels, which is also Word's practical limit for one numbering
          definition.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Opening the result elsewhere</h2>
      <p>
        The file is standard OOXML, so it opens in Microsoft Word (2007 onwards), LibreOffice Writer,
        Google Docs, Apple Pages and other apps that read .docx. Google Docs and LibreOffice map
        Word's built-in style names to their own, so headings stay headings there too.
      </p>
      <p>
        For a PDF, use <a href="/markdown-to-pdf">Markdown to PDF</a>. For HTML, use
        <a href="/markdown-to-html">Markdown to HTML</a>. For the other direction,
        <a href="/docx-to-markdown">DOCX to Markdown</a> converts .docx back to Markdown.
      </p>
    </section>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
